node_modules

# Output
build-artifacts
.output
.vercel
.netlify
.wrangler
/.svelte-kit
/build

# Store screenshots
#
# The tooling in tools/screenshots/ is committed; its output is not. Every shot
# carries live data — the countdown on each row, today's meeting list, the
# clean-time total — so all 32 change on every capture whether the UI moved or
# not. Committing them would add ~13MB of fresh blobs per release to a public
# repository, with no delta compression and a 32-file diff that never means
# anything. `npm run screenshots` rebuilds the set in about fifteen minutes, and
# App Store Connect and the Play Console keep the images actually uploaded.
#
# Scoped to the four capture directories rather than the whole tree, so that
# play/feature-graphic.png stays in git: it is listing artwork, not a
# screenshot, nothing here regenerates it, and this is the only copy that is not
# sitting on one machine's desktop.
store/screenshots/app-store/**/*.png
store/screenshots/play/phone/*.png
store/screenshots/play/tablet-10in/*.png

# Test and tooling output
/coverage
*.tsbuildinfo
*.log
npm-debug.log*

# Local working notes — review write-ups, scratch findings. Deliberately not
# committed: they go stale against the code they describe, and anything in one
# worth keeping belongs in AGENTS.md or an issue instead.
/REVIEW.md

# OS
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Signing material — belt and braces for a public repository.
#
# None of this belongs in git under any circumstances: a private key or an App
# Store Connect key in a public repo is compromised the moment a scraper reaches
# it, and Apple caps distribution certificates at three per team, so a leaked one
# is expensive to replace. Keep it outside every repo and hand it to CI as a
# secret. These patterns exist so an absent-minded `git add -A` cannot do the
# wrong thing; the real defence is not putting the files here in the first place.
*.p12
*.p8
*.cer
*.certSigningRequest
*.mobileprovision
*.keystore
*.jks
keystore.properties
android/keystore.properties

# Env
#
# The Google Maps browser key is a PUBLIC_ var — it ships in the bundle and is
# visible to anyone who opens devtools. It lives in .env rather than in source so
# it can be rotated and restricted (HTTP referrer + bundle ID) without a code
# change, and so forks bring their own key and their own billing.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
!.env.release.example
!.env.test

# Vite
vite.config.js.timestamp-*
vite.config.ts.timestamp-*

# Native projects
#
# ios/ and android/ ARE committed. They hold hand-written configuration that
# `npx cap add` does not regenerate — the Google Maps API key wiring, the
# location-permission usage strings in Info.plist, and the ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION
# permissions in AndroidManifest.xml. Ignoring them would silently discard that
# setup on a fresh clone. Only the build products are ignored.
ios/App/App/public/
ios/App/Pods/
ios/App/build/
ios/App/output/
ios/App/App/capacitor.config.json
ios/DerivedData/
ios/.build/
*.xcuserdatad
xcuserdata/

android/app/src/main/assets/public/
android/app/src/main/assets/capacitor.config.json
android/app/src/main/assets/capacitor.plugins.json
android/app/build/
android/build/
android/.gradle/
android/.idea/
android/local.properties
android/capacitor-cordova-android-plugins/
